{"team":"NE","col":"#7FA8C9","identity":"run-heavy","funnel":"balanced-D","plays_pg":60.2,"adot":8.4,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>A Ground-First Machine</h2><p>There's no bell-cow to bank on \u2014 the lead back sees just <strong>50%</strong> of carries (league ~59%), a committee that caps any one back's ceiling and makes their rushing props matchup- and script-dependent.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>NE throw by design. They pass <strong>-0%</strong> over expectation (league -2%) and keep firing even with a lead (39% early-down pass when ahead vs ~41%) \u2014 the kind of pass-first identity that keeps receivers live in every script.</p><p><strong>Hunter Henry</strong> is a genuine centerpiece, not an afterthought \u2014 <strong>18%</strong> of targets (league ~15%).</p><p><strong>Drake Maye</strong> is a scramble threat \u2014 he takes off on <strong>5.8%</strong> of dropbacks (nearly double the 3.3% norm), rushing production that both lifts his own yardage and siphons a few carries from the backfield.</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Inside the 20 they lean run-heavy, passing <strong>51%</strong> of the time, funnelling touchdown equity to <strong>Rhamondre Stevenson</strong>. And with the TE commanding 18% of targets, <strong>Hunter Henry</strong> is a live red-zone option too.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 A Balanced Defense</h2><p>They lock down opposing WR1s \u2014 just 50 rec yds/game (vs ~58); fade receiving props there.</p><p>They surrender <strong>114 rushing</strong> and <strong>210 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). No dominant funnel \u2014 read it matchup by matchup.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: back <strong>Rhamondre Stevenson</strong>'s rushing volume in favored/positive scripts.</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; the profile rebuilds through the season as the current staff and roster set their own tendencies.</em></p></div>","depth":{"QB":[{"name":"Drake Maye","slot":"QB1","starter":true,"td":19,"props